{
    "name": "Map-entered variables",
    "views": [
        {
            "type": "widget",
            "sub_type": "toc",
            "items": [
                "Introduction",
                "MEV and MUXs",
                "Minimization method"
            ]
        },
        {
            "type": "text",
            "size": "H1",
            "content": "Introduction",
            "scrollToId": 0
        },
        {
            "type": "text",
            "size": "H3",
            "content": "Maps with entered variables are Karnaugh Maps which can have Boolean expression in its cells, not just ones and zeros. These maps are also referred to as Variable-Entered Maps. However, in most literature the MEV acronym is used."
        },
        {
            "type": "text",
            "size": "H3",
            "content": "These maps are useful to represent functions with large number of variables in a map of lower order. They are also used to describe flip-flop's excitation functions."
        },
        {
            "type": "text",
            "size": "H3",
            "content": "For instance, given the three-variables Boolean function $f(x,y,z)$ described by the following truth-table:"
        },
        {
            "type": "widget",
            "sub_type": "table",
            "content": {
                "heading": [
                    ": Table 1: Simple MEV example {: .tblcap} :"
                ],
                "rows": [
                    [
                        "$x$",
                        "$y$",
                        "$z$",
                        "$f(x,y,z)$",
                        "MEV"
                    ],
                    [
                        "0",
                        "0",
                        "0",
                        "0",
                        "$z$"
                    ],
                    [
                        "0",
                        "0",
                        "1",
                        "1",
                        "^^"
                    ],
                    [
                        "0",
                        "1",
                        "0",
                        "1",
                        "$\\overline{z}$"
                    ],
                    [
                        "0",
                        "1",
                        "1",
                        "0",
                        "^^"
                    ],
                    [
                        "1",
                        "0",
                        "0",
                        "0",
                        "$z$"
                    ],
                    [
                        "1",
                        "0",
                        "1",
                        "1",
                        "^^"
                    ],
                    [
                        "1",
                        "1",
                        "0",
                        "0",
                        "0"
                    ],
                    [
                        "1",
                        "1",
                        "1",
                        "0",
                        "^^"
                    ]
                ]
            }
        },
        {
            "type": "text",
            "size": "H3",
            "content": "The column labelled MEV shows the Boolean relation between $f(x,y,z)$ and $z$ for each of the four possible combinations of $(x,y)$."
        },
        {
            "type": "text",
            "size": "H3",
            "content": "This function is represented by the map:"
        },
        {
            "type": "widget",
            "sub_type": "table",
            "content": {
                "heading": [
                    ": Table 2: MEV for function defined by Table 1 :"
                ],
                "rows": [
                    [
                        "",
                        "$\\boldsymbol{y}$"
                    ],
                    [
                        "$\\boldsymbol{x}$",
                        "0",
                        "1"
                    ],
                    [
                        "0",
                        "$z$",
                        "$\\overline{z}$"
                    ],
                    [
                        "1",
                        "$z$",
                        "0"
                    ]
                ]
            }
        },
        {
            "type": "text",
            "size": "H3",
            "content": "The MEV can be optimized directly, just like a Karnaugh Map but the process is more complex."
        },
        {
            "type": "text",
            "size": "H1",
            "content": "MEV and MUXs",
            "scrollToId": 1
        },
        {
            "type": "text",
            "size": "H3",
            "content": "A MEV can be directly implemented in a MUX, where the function of each cell must be connected to the corresponding data line of the MUX, while the map's variables are connected to the selection lines."
        },
        {
            "type": "text",
            "size": "H3",
            "content": "For instance, the example from Table 2 is implemented in the interactive circuit below:"
        },
        {
            "type": "widget",
            "sub_type": "image",
            "content": {
                "link": "https://circuitverse.org/simulator/embed/simple-mev"
            }
        },
        {
            "type": "text",
            "size": "H1",
            "content": "Minimization method",
            "scrollToId": 2
        },
        {
            "type": "text",
            "size": "H3",
            "content": "A method of MEV minimization based on partial variables is described in:"
        },
        {
            "type": "widget",
            "sub_type": "bullet_list",
            "items": [
                "Section \"Variable-Entered Maps\" in [1]",
                "Section \"Minimizing MEV maps\" in [2]",
                "Section \"Map-entered variables\" in [3]"
            ]
        },
        {
            "type": "text",
            "size": "H3",
            "content": "Another recursive method which can be used when the MEV only has expressions of one variable (or its complement) is described next."
        },
        {
            "type": "text",
            "size": "H3",
            "content": "The expressions found in the map's cells can have these forms:"
        },
        {
            "type": "widget",
            "sub_type": "bullet_list",
            "items": [
                "0s or 1s",
                "$z$ or $\\overline{z}$",
                "$\\phi$ (don't care)",
                "$z\\phi$ or $\\overline{z}\\phi$",
                "$z+\\overline{z}\\phi$ or $\\overline{z}+z\\phi$"
            ]
        },
        {
            "type": "text",
            "size": "H3",
            "content": "The process has two phases"
        },
        {
            "type": "text",
            "size": "H2",
            "content": "Phase 1: Coverage"
        },
        {
            "type": "text",
            "size": "H3",
            "content": "Let $D$ and $\\overline{D}$ (its complement) be the MEV variable, then ones correspond to $1\\rightarrow D+\\overline{D}$."
        },
        {
            "type": "text",
            "size": "H3",
            "content": "In this phase, the goal is to obtain the largest grouping or coverage for both the variable and its complement, independently."
        },
        {
            "type": "widget",
            "sub_type": "numbered_list",
            "items": [
                {
                    "content": "Start by covering the variables alone."
                },
                {
                    "content": "Expand the coverage to identical adjacent variables."
                },
                {
                    "content": "Expand the coverage by incorporating ones (1s)."
                },
                {
                    "content": "Expand the coverage by incorporating cells containing don't cares."
                },
                {
                    "content": "Repeat from step 2 to extend the coverage as much as possible"
                }
            ]
        },
        {
            "type": "text",
            "size": "H3",
            "content": "The Boolean product expressions of the groups will be part of the final sum."
        },
        {
            "type": "text",
            "size": "H2",
            "content": "Phase 2: Transformation"
        },
        {
            "type": "text",
            "size": "H3",
            "content": "The goal of this phase is to discard the MEV variables, since they have already been covered in Phase 1:"
        },
        {
            "type": "widget",
            "sub_type": "numbered_list",
            "items": [
                {
                    "content": "MEV variables $D$ and $\\overline{D}$ are replaced by 0."
                },
                {
                    "content": "Cells with 0s and $\\phi$ remain the same."
                },
                {
                    "content": "Cells with 1s become $\\phi$ if both parts $D$ and $\\overline{D}$ were covered. Otherwise they remain as 1s"
                },
                {
                    "content": "Cells containing $z\\phi$ or $\\overline{z}\\phi$ become 0s."
                },
                {
                    "content": "Cells with the expressions $z+\\overline{z}\\phi$ and $\\overline{z}+z\\phi$ become $\\phi$ if the term not multiplied by $\\phi$ has been covered. Otherwise they become 1s."
                }
            ]
        },
        {
            "type": "text",
            "size": "H3",
            "content": "The new map is a normal Karnaugh Map with 1s and 0s, and can be minimised accordingly. The new product terms are added to those found in Phase 1."
        }
    ]
}
